 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "What it is",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "A Hall-effect switch that answers to either magnetic pole: a strong enough south or north field on the branded face turns the output on (low); with no field present the output stays off. One die holds the Hall plate, an amplifier with chopper offset cancellation, a sample-and-hold, a latching stage and a MOSFET that sinks the output current. The A3213 and A3214 are the same part except for duty cycle and therefore average supply current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sampled operation and power",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "An internal timer wakes the chip for about 60 µs per period and keeps it asleep for the rest: the period is 240 µs typ on the A3213 and 60 ms typ on the A3214. The field is sampled and latched at the end of each awake pulse; the output holds that state through the sleep time. Supply current is IDD(EN) while awake and IDD(DIS) while asleep, and does not depend on the output state. Typical average power at 2.75 V is 825 µW (A3213) and 14 µW (A3214)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output switching",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The output turns on (low) once the field normal to the Hall element exceeds BOPS (south) or drops below BOPN (north), and can then sink up to 1 mA at VOUT(ON). It turns off (high) once the field drops below BRPS or exceeds BRPN. The difference between operate and release points is the hysteresis Bhys, so external vibration and electrical noise do not make the output chatter. Between BRPN and BRPS the output is off; the off level is 5 V max."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Chopper stabilization",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The Hall plate behaves like a resistor bridge whose mismatch gives an offset, worsened by overmolding, temperature and package stress. A high-frequency clock (fC, 340 kHz typ) drives CMOS switches that reverse the bias current direction through the plate while keeping the field-induced voltage; a sample-and-hold recovers that voltage, and low-offset bipolar circuitry processes it. Quiescent output stays stable over temperature and recovers precisely after temperature cycling; output repeatability is slightly reduced. The sampling frequency is high so that faster signals can be followed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bypass capacitor",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Allegro strongly recommends a capacitor from VDD to GND mounted right beside the device. It reduces noise from outside and noise produced by the chopper; a battery supply, being of relatively high impedance, needs it all the more."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Magnet",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Bringing either end of a bar magnet near the branded face is the simplest way to actuate it; the output polarity is the same for either pole."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature ranges and packages",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Temperature code E is -40 °C to 85 °C, L is -40 °C to 150 °C. LH is a low-profile 3-pin SOT23W for surface mount; UA is a 3-pin SIP for through hole, in matrix HD style (recommended for new design) or chopper style (not for new design). All are lead-free with a 100 % matte-tin leadframe (suffix -T). The parts are AEC-Q100 qualified."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power derating",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Maximum allowable VDD stays at 5.5 V up to about 165 °C for all three board and package cases. Allowed dissipation falls linearly from the 20 °C value to zero near 165 °C with slope set by RθJA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Marking",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "LH and matrix-style UA are marked NNT: the last three digits of the part number and a temperature code letter; the UA also carries the supplier emblem. The chopper-style UA uses the last two digits."
  }
 ],
